1.37.0 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Ozon tab stops keeping a currency of its own. One shop is one legal entity is one currency: a Belarusian entity gives a .by domain, a storefront in BYN and a cabinet in BYN, and a Polish shop will be its own instance with its own entity and its own złoty. So `ozon_settings.currency` was a second copy of an answer the shop already had, and the whole apparatus around it — two constants, a validator, a setter, a field in the request and in the tab, two message keys, and yesterday's refusal to run the markup ladder across currencies — existed to keep two truths in step. Wildberries never had any of it and never needed it: its price carries no currency at all, the cabinet decides. Prices now go out labelled with the shop's own money, and the cabinet's currency, read on «Проверить связь», became what it should have been — a check that says which of the two is wrong

1.36.1 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Prices go to Ozon to the kopeck. We rounded up to a whole unit before sending — 25.11 left as 26.00 — on the belief that the platform takes whole rubles. It does not: "25.11" is accepted and stored as 25.11, verified against a live BYN cabinet. On roubles the rounding cost a kopeck; on a 2.53 BYN item it added eighteen percent to the buyer's price under the seller's name. The row now remembers exactly what was sent, so nothing flaps between "needs push" and "pushed"

1.36.0 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The markup ladder refuses to cross currencies. It multiplies the shelf price to get the platform price — it does not convert, and nothing said so: a storefront trading in RUB against an ozon.by cabinet in BYN would have shipped prices off by the exchange rate, about thirtyfold, in the direction that sells the goods for nothing. Both bulk fills now stop and say which two currencies disagree. A price typed into a single row is untouched: the owner typing it knows which money they mean. We do not convert, because a rate carried in the shop goes stale in silence

1.35.0 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- «Отправить сейчас» no longer waits out a backoff the owner cannot see. A refused price or stock is retried later on purpose — that is what backoff is for, and the five-minute ticker keeps honouring it — but the button is an explicit instruction from someone who has just fixed what the platform complained about. It answered "sent 0, failed 0" and looked broken. Measured on a live cabinet: a price refused with «Неверно указана валюта» sat for thirteen minutes while the button did nothing
- The warehouse field says what one warehouse means. A cabinet may hold several, and a product carries a single stock figure — so the shop sends that figure to one of them and cannot split it. Stating it beats letting the owner work it out from an oversell

1.34.1 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The warning added an hour ago sent the owner to a dropdown that can be empty. Neither platform creates warehouses over the API — we only read the list — so a cabinet with no warehouse at all leaves nothing to pick, and that is the case a first-time seller is actually in. The message now names both halves: pick one above, and if the list is empty the warehouse has to be created in the platform's own cabinet first

1.34.0 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- «Связать по артикулу» is gone from the code, not just from the screen. It read the cabinet's articles and wrote every pair that matched — which is what «Опубликовать» does over a selection instead of over everything, and the shop has had multi-select on that table since it was built. The endpoint, its response types and its route are removed; the matching rules its tests held (a multi-size card is refused with a reason, an imported "article-size" matches its own barcode) now run against publishing, which is the code that does the matching
- Publishing tells you when the stock cannot travel. A link is written the moment articles match, but stock reaches Ozon and Wildberries only from a warehouse, and an owner who never picked one saw "published" and then nothing at all on the platform: the worker skips stocks in silence, and silence is not an answer

1.33.1 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The channel tabs stop saying the same thing three times. Alongside the new state buttons sat a «Связывание» block with a button that did what «Опубликовать» already does — both read the cabinet's articles and write the pairs that match, one over a selection and one over the whole catalogue — and its own counters called two states one thing: "без связи: 105" for 104 products with no card and one that only needed a click. That is the exact conflation the state buttons exist to undo, printed twenty centimetres below them
- Cards in the cabinet matching no product of ours now have their own section, filled by the cabinet call the tab already makes when it opens. Before, that list appeared only after pressing the button that is gone
- The summary line above the buttons keeps only the number they do not carry: how many cards the cabinet holds

1.33.0 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Ozon and Wildberries tabs open on the products the owner can actually do something with, instead of on the whole catalogue. The previous release taught every row to say which of four states it was in; the table still listed all 24 000 of them, so finding the seven with a card meant scrolling past 23 801 without one. The states are now buttons carrying their own counts — «Можно связать 7 · Связано 42 · Нет карточки 23 801 · Все товары 24 000» — and the tab opens on the first one that has an action attached
- Which state a product is in is the platform's answer, not ours, so the filter travels as the ids the tab learned when it opened. That keeps the promise the cabinet call was built on: one request per tab open, never one per page of a hundred rows
- Cards in the cabinet that match no product of ours are now named, not only counted. Twenty of them, which is enough to recognise what they are; the count already answered whether they matter

1.32.3 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- robots.txt tells Yandex which query parameters reorder a page rather than make a new one. A canonical already pointed every sorted variant at the plain address, but a canonical is a hint given after the fetch, so the crawler kept spending a request on each permutation. Yandex reported the waste on a live shop: four sort orders crossed with an in-stock filter mean the sorted copies of a section outnumber its real pages, on a catalogue of 24 000 products
- Clean-param lists sort, desc and instock. It deliberately does not list page: page two holds different goods, and folding it into page one would hide most of the catalogue from the crawler — a test pins that

1.32.2 / August 27, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The product table stops claiming a product has no photo when it has one. Each 40px cell pulled the full-size original, and there are up to 500 rows on a page: a hundred products meant 9.8 MB of WebP requested at once, the browser dropped two thirds of them, and the error handler replaced every dropped one with the "no photo" stub. The table showed 67 products as pictureless while all 100 had a picture
- It now asks for the small copy the storefront has used all along, and falls back to the original before the stub — a photo uploaded before thumbnails existed has no small copy, and going straight to the stub would repeat the same lie. Rows load lazily on top of that
- The storefront was already right: its grid has taken thumbnails since they existed, for exactly this reason. The admin was simply never given the same treatment, and it needs it more — a 40px cell against a 220px card, 500 rows against 60

1.32.1 / August 25, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The admin is reachable on a phone again. Its header is a single row of eight tabs and three links, and a row that cannot wrap stretched the whole page to 999px on a 390px screen; the overflow was not scrollable, so the price column, half the tabs and the link back to the shop sat past the right edge with no way to reach them. The filter row above the product table did the same with a 256px search field beside a 192px select
- Both rows now wrap, and the two fields may shrink below their preferred width. Measured on a live admin at 390px: the page went from 999px to 390px and every control in the header became reachable. Nothing changes on a desktop, where the rows fit on one line and never wrap
- The product table itself was never the problem: it lives in a container of its own that scrolls sideways, which is what a table of twenty columns should do on a phone

1.32.0 / August 23, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A product page can offer to order in a message. Fill in a Telegram handle or a WhatsApp number and each card grows a button that opens the buyer's own messenger with the text already written: what they want, its article, its price and the page it came from. The seller gets a contact they can answer; the buyer skips a checkout they did not want
- It is a link, not a widget. A messenger deep link needs no JavaScript, so the storefront's promise survives intact — and so does the page weight. The whole message is escaped on the server, because a real title reads `Ерш унитазный с/подст "Шляпа" д13х36см` and an unescaped quote ends the address at the first one
- Nothing about that button is counted. No click counter, no marker in the text, no row in the database: a deal closed in a chat is the seller's business, and a shop that quietly measured it would be charging rent on a conversation it had no part in
- The two product feeds are now documented rather than merely present. `/yml.xml` feeds a Yandex Direct product campaign and `/gmc.xml` feeds Google Merchant Center; both are built from the live catalogue, carry article, price, currency, availability, category, picture and description per offer, and need no export step. Measured on a live shop: 23,844 offers and, because a variant here is its own product, 23,844 distinct addresses — the duplicate-URL problem that afflicts most feeds cannot arise

1.31.0 / August 21, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The storefront has a contacts page. A shop is expected to say whom the buyer is paying, and this one had nowhere to say it: only the delivery-and-payment page existed. Both search engines weigh a published contact when they rank a shop, and a buyer about to pay a stranger looks for it first
- It asks the owner for nothing: the phone and the legal details are already in settings, so a shop whose footer is filled gets the page for free. It appears only when there is something on it, and it is listed in the sitemap, in llms.txt and in the footer exactly when it exists
- The contact address is on it too. It doubles as the admin login, which is the reason to hesitate, but the secret is the password, not the address a buyer needs to reach the shop
- Guessing that password is now slowed down. The login had no brake of any kind: bcrypt alone allows on the order of a million attempts a night. After three failures each answer waits, the wait doubles, and it stops at eight seconds. Deliberately a delay and not a lockout — a shop has one owner and nobody to call, so refusing after N tries would let anyone who knows their address shut them out of their own admin until somebody reached the server over SSH. The wait is served before the password is checked, so its length never says whether the guess was right

1.30.0 / August 21, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Search matches every word a buyer typed, in any order, instead of the exact string with their exact spacing. Measured on a live catalogue: "эмалированная кастрюля" found 1 product and now finds 302, "зонт романтика" and "ерш унитазный" found none and now find 4 and 16. One word behaves exactly as it did, and a query whose words are not all present still finds nothing
- The cost is one pass over the catalogue per word rather than one in total: at 46 ms a pass on 24 000 products, a three-word query stays well inside a page load
- A word of one or two characters still matches almost anything, because each word is matched as a substring rather than as a word: "кастрюля 3 л" returns 338 products where four are meant. It returned none before, so this is an improvement and not a fix. Word boundaries, and the ranking this has never had, are what a full-text index brings when substring matching stops being enough
- The Ozon and Wildberries tabs say which products can actually be published. They project onto cards the seller already created in their own cabinet, and until now the table listed the whole catalogue without saying which rows had one: on a live shop that is 24 000 products against a few dozen cards, so the owner ticked a hundred and got ninety-nine refusals back, and the integration read as broken when every part of it worked
- Opening a tab now asks the platform once and matches it against the catalogue: the header says how many cards the cabinet holds, how many products are linked, how many are ready to link and how many have no card, and each row says which of those it is. Wildberries carries a fourth answer of its own — a card with several sizes, which one article cannot link — counted apart, because telling that owner to create a card would make a duplicate
- The question is asked when the tab opens and not once per page of the table, and the answer is not cached: a stored one would go stale exactly when the owner has just created the card they came to look for

1.29.0 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The product table shows when a row last changed, and sorts by it. After an import or a run of AI rewrites over twenty thousand products, "what actually moved" is the only question worth asking, and until now the answer was nowhere on the screen. The date was already stored and already published as lastmod in the sitemap; it simply never reached the admin
- Two notes for whoever picks up the channel tabs and search next live in docs/: what the Ozon and Wildberries tabs really do (they project onto existing cards, they do not publish) and why their table misleads on a large catalogue; and what the search does today, measured, with the two ways forward and what each costs

1.28.2 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Search finds goods whose names are capitalised. SQLite folds case for Latin letters only, so a shop whose catalogue is written in Russian was invisible to anyone typing in lower case: on a live shop "кастрюля" returned 8 products and "Кастрюля" returned 521 — the same 521 a buyer never saw. Both sides of the comparison now go through case folding that knows Unicode. Measured at 46 ms over 24 000 products, the same full pass as before, only correct

1.28.1 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- What the shop already knows about a parcel now travels with a rewrite request, so a card can state the weight and the packed size instead of leaving them out. They are sent in the units they are stored in and converted for the text on the service's side: a model asked to divide by a thousand is a model given the one chance to be wrong about a number a courier charges for

1.28.0 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- An import from Ozon or Wildberries now brings the weight and the parcel size with it. Both platforms make them mandatory on a card, so a seller who already trades there has them filled in — and the shop was throwing them away on parse. This is the only way a catalogue of twenty thousand ever gets weighed: nobody types that in by hand, and a model cannot read a weight that is written nowhere
- Units are the platform's and differ, so they are converted once, on the way in: Ozon states its own beside the number, Wildberries fixes centimetres and kilograms by contract, and the shop stores grams and millimetres. A unit nobody recognises yields nothing rather than a guess — a weight wrong by a factor of a thousand is worse than a missing one, and it is silent
- A refresh fills what is empty and leaves alone what the owner set, the same rule the category follows: a platform's weight is a starting point, and whoever corrected one is the person who actually weighed the parcel

1.27.0 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A product can now carry its weight and the size of its parcel. Optional and typed: unstated stays unstated rather than becoming a zero, because a delivery quote that reads an unweighed product as weightless is wrong in a way nobody notices until the parcel is handed over. Both are shown on the storefront as a characteristics block — kilograms and centimetres for the buyer — and in the page's markup in the units they are stored in, and both appear only when the owner has stated them
- Characteristics beyond those have a place too: a product carries them as a set of named values, and a definition says what each one is — its type, the values it may take, and the caption a buyer reads. Types are checked in one place, so "number" is a promise rather than a comment: a weight in words, a colour outside its list and a field nobody declared are all refused at the door. The shape is the one fastometa already uses, so a client that reads one reads the other
- The article of an imported product, its text and now its measurements all survive a price-list refresh untouched — the feed owns the price and the stock, the owner owns everything else

1.26.2 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The product's own section is always among the ones offered to the model, however deep in the tree it sits. Bounding the list by size had left the deep sections out, so the model could not answer "keep the one it has" — and the shallow section it picked instead would have replaced a precise path with a vague one

1.26.1 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The AI button failed on a big catalogue and would not say why. The list of sections offered to the model was capped at two hundred paths, and on a 24 000-product tree those two hundred came to 55 KB — enough to push the answer out of the model's context, so it returned nothing in three seconds. The list is now bounded by size and filled with the shallowest sections first
- When the service refuses, it says so in its own words instead of a blanket "try again", and the reason goes to the log. Diagnosing the failure above took a shell on the server, which is exactly what a log is for
- The button reads as a button: an outlined accent rather than a cancel-grey one, with a spinner while the text is being written

1.26.0 / August 20, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A product card can be rewritten by a model: "Improve the text (AI)" turns the supplier's warehouse shorthand into a name and a description a person reads, and suggests a section from the ones the shop already has. Inventing properties is forbidden in the prompt, and a section the shop did not offer is dropped rather than created — the worst the model can do is stay silent. The draft goes into the form, not into the database: the owner reads it, edits it and saves it, and closing the dialog changes nothing. The service is paid for separately with an AdHunters key entered in the profile; without a key the button is not shown, and the shop itself charges nothing
- A re-import can no longer take back what the owner wrote. It never could — a feed refresh only ever wrote price, stock and an empty category — but the whole point of a paid rewrite is that it survives next week's price list, so a test now holds the two apart
- The article of an imported product is read-only. It is the key a feed matches by: change it and the next import finds no match, creates a duplicate and zeroes the original's stock, silently and a week later. Goods with no supplier have no feed to break and stay editable

1.25.0 / August 19, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop hands AI assistants its own map: /llms.txt, generated from the live catalogue — the shop's name, how an order works, the sections with counts, where the sitemap is. Answer engines send buyers, and an assistant decides from one read, not from twenty thousand pages
- A checkout refused for a missing contact no longer wipes the form: the typed name and comment come back filled, and the error moved next to the fields it talks about instead of the top of the page
- The order form autofills on a phone: name, phone and email fields now carry autocomplete attributes, and the sample phone number follows the shop's own currency — +375 in a shop that sells in roubles of Belarus, +7 elsewhere

1.24.2 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Import and recompute now round a price identically. The Go path rounded the cost to whole kopecks before the markup, the SQL path rounded once at the end — a kopeck apart on almost half a live catalogue, which the import's "Check" then reported as thousands of changed prices that never moved. A test now holds the two formulas together
- "Recompute prices" first saves the fields on the screen. It used to reprice the catalogue by the stored ladder while the owner was looking at the numbers they had just typed, and report success
- A ladder folded shut no longer hides that it is a ladder: the band editor shows whenever the ladder has bands, and deleting them down to one row brings the plain percent field back. There is no state where a multi-band markup prices the catalogue with nothing on screen admitting it

1.24.1 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- "Recompute prices" failed on a single markup band — the plain-percentage case the new Prices block writes by default — with an internal error, because CASE with no WHEN is a syntax error in SQLite. Caught within minutes of the release going live; no price had moved, the UPDATE never ran

1.24.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Prices are two fields now, not three numbers spread over two tabs under the same name. Products has a "Prices" block: a cost rate that turns a supplier's price into the shop's money, and a markup percentage on top — the ladder that used to be the only way to set a markup is still there, folded behind "A different markup for cheap and dear goods", for the sieve-and-steamer case where one percentage cannot serve both. A line under the fields spells out a real row's own chain: cost, times the rate, times the markup, equals the shelf price
- The Import tab lost the ladder and the recompute button — both moved to where the prices they produce are shown. Its own rate field is gone too, except when a feed's currency does not match the shop's; a rouble price list into a rouble shop no longer asks the owner to think about a coefficient at all
- Ozon and Wildberries read as one product again: Ozon's publish table lived inside a form's narrow column and wrapped titles onto five lines, WB's did not. Both now use the full-width layout, the platform column carries the same name in both, and the section heading is the same weight

1.23.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Photos in the product dialog can be dragged into any order. The first one is the card in search results, in the catalogue and in a marketplace listing, and an imported catalogue arrives in whatever order the supplier's feed had — often with a dead link first. The new order is saved as it is dropped, not on "Save": it belongs to the product, not to the form
- The second scrollbar beside a dialog is gone, and this time it was measured rather than guessed at: the page was already locked, but the dialog element carries the browser's own overflow:auto and its padding plus the card's height added up to 38 pixels more than it could show
- The Import tab shows the coefficient the catalogue actually runs on instead of a hard-coded 1. Pressing "Recompute prices" there would have multiplied twenty thousand prices by twenty-one

1.22.2 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The offer in a product's markup now carries the two things the shop actually knows and search engines ask for: the condition of the goods and how long the price holds. Without a validity date a price is eventually treated as stale and dropped from the snippet, which is how a shop loses the line that made it worth clicking. Availability was already there — the stars next to a competitor's result are not markup we are missing but reviews we do not have, and inventing them costs the snippet outright

1.22.1 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The page behind a dialog is finally still. It was locked on the body, while the scrollbar belongs to the root — so the list kept scrolling under the card and the window carried two scrollbars side by side
- A photo in the product dialog that fails to load shows the shop's mark, like everywhere else. The list had this already; the dialog, where the owner actually looks at the photos, did not

1.22.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop has a markup ladder of its own, on the Import tab beside the coefficient: up to which cost which multiplier. One number over a whole catalogue lies at both ends — on a 7-rouble sieve it leaves nothing, and on a 300-rouble steamer it prices the shop above the brand's own store. Both were measured against live competitors before this was written. The coefficient keeps its job, carrying the supplier's price into the shop's money; the ladder adds the margin on top, so the bands are set in the money the owner sells in
- Saving the ladder moves no price. It applies where a price change belongs — under "Recompute prices" — and the import and its "Check" preview read the same rule, so what the preview promises is what the catalogue gets
- /favicon.ico is answered. Crawlers ask for it before they read a line of the page, and Google puts that icon next to the snippet: a 404 there is why a shop shows a globe in the results while its competitors show their own mark

1.21.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- An order line shows the goods it sold: a photo, the article, and the title as a link to the product's own page. Checking what somebody actually ordered no longer means searching the catalogue by hand. Both the photo and the link are looked up when the order is opened, never stored — the order's snapshot must not age with the catalogue, and goods deleted since simply stop being a link
- The order card reads like a card: the buyer under a heading with their phone and email as links, the comment in a block of its own, a line per item with its own sum, and the status right there, so an order can be closed without going back to the list
- Sizes in the admin read as sizes again: 32.4 MB, 3 GB, 152 KB. The browser's own compact notation turned a disk into "1,2 млрд Б" in Russian — arithmetically true and unreadable. KB, MB and GB are technical marks and stay in Latin in every language, as do CPU, RAM and HDD
- A changed price needs nothing submitted to a search engine, and now a test says so: the storefront sends no caching headers for a page whose price can move, the markup carries the same number the shopper sees, and the sitemap dates the change so a crawler returns on its own

1.20.2 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The browser's own "broken image" glyph is gone for good: a picture that fails to load is covered by its own mask, which a loaded picture never shows. Moving the glyph out of the frame did not work — it is drawn inside the picture's box and ignores colour, font size and indentation alike
- A dialog freezes the page behind it. A modal takes the clicks but not the wheel, so the list kept scrolling under the card and the window carried two scrollbars side by side
- A price in the product list no longer breaks across two lines, with the currency stranded under the number
- The warning mark for a supplier-hosted photo is back to the one photo a shopper actually sees in the listing. Lit by any of a product's photos it burned on every row of a catalogue that is hotlinked from end to end, and a warning that fires on everything warns of nothing

1.20.1 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The browser's own "broken image" glyph no longer shows in the corner of a tile whose photo did not load: the shop's mark now stands there alone
- The thumbnails under a product's gallery carry the mark too. A strip of blank white squares told the owner nothing about which of the twelve photos is the missing one

1.20.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A photo that fails to load now shows the shop's own "no photo" mark instead of the product's title sprawled across the tile — in the catalogue, on the product page and in the admin's list alike. The frame around every picture carries the mark, and a picture that loads covers it; the storefront does this without a line of JavaScript, which it still does not have
- Nothing is deleted over a photo any more. A supplier's link that answers 404 today can answer perfectly tomorrow — their server comes back, and the shop shows the picture again by itself, with no row to restore. Yesterday's rule that dropped such rows, and the separate link-checking pass added hours ago, are both gone: the display was the problem, and the display is where it is solved

1.19.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- "Check the photos": a pass that asks the supplier which of the hotlinked pictures are still there and drops the ones that are gone, downloading nothing. "Fill in photos" already did this, but on its way to copying every picture onto your disk — and a shop that has not agreed with its supplier about their materials still cannot afford a dead link, which the catalogue renders as the product title sprawled where the photo belongs. Measured on a live catalogue of 69 905 supplier links, about one in twenty five is dead
- What counts as gone is decided in one place for both passes, so the download and the check can never disagree about which photo is a corpse

1.18.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- An order opens as a card: article, product, unit price, quantity and line sum for every line, the buyer's comment in full, and their phone and email as links to call and write from. Until now the whole order was one joined line of titles, and the article and unit price — both kept in the order's own snapshot — never reached the screen
- The order list stopped being set by the longest title in it. One supplier name of two hundred characters made a three-line row and pushed the total half a screen away from the goods it belonged to; a title now takes two lines at most and the rest is in the card
- A phone number no longer breaks in the middle across two lines, and a buyer's comment is visible in the row instead of only in a tooltip nobody hovers over
- The order total is counted by the shop, once. The browser and the accountant's CSV each parsed the stored snapshot their own way, which is how two numbers for one order appear; the raw snapshot now stays on the server, where it is the legal record it was meant to be
- The CSV for the accountant gained the buyer's comment: delivery wishes and a convenient time to call were the one thing the export dropped

1.17.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop keeps a log file and hands it to its owner: Status → Expert mode shows its size, when it last moved, and opens it. Until now everything the background did — a photo that would not download, an order email that never left — went to the machine's journal, which the owner of a hosted shop has no way to read. The path is a config key (log_path), and the file is capped the way our other services cap theirs
- A photo the supplier has deleted is dropped instead of retried forever. An imported photo is a link, that link is the only copy, and a dead one made the catalogue render the product's title sprawling where the picture should be; without the row the storefront falls back to its own "no photo" mark. Measured on a live catalogue of 69 905 supplier links, about one in twenty five
- Only 404 and 410 count as gone. A refusal to hotlink, a rate limit, a bad hour at the supplier and an error page served as a photo all keep the link and are tried again — a wider net would erase live photos during somebody else's outage, and there is nothing to restore them from
- "Fill in photos" now reports three numbers: downloaded, gone, still links
- The warning mark on a product now lights up when any of its photos is still on the supplier's server, not only the first one. A product with one photo of its own and two borrowed looked settled

1.16.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Wildberries markup ladder got its "Remove" button back. Ozon has had one since the tab was written, and without it a band typed by mistake could only be undone by reloading the page
- Dialogs are the browser's own now: Escape closes them, focus stays inside while they are open, and a screen reader announces them as dialogs. The admin's dialogs had none of that
- Signing in and the first-run setup are real forms: the browser offers the saved password, Enter submits, and the email field is an email field on a phone keyboard
- Confirming "Fill in photos" is one question instead of a dialog holding a single checkbox
- Uploading a price list no longer walks the file byte by byte in the browser; a 143 MB spreadsheet is handed over by the platform instead
- Disk and database sizes in the admin are formatted by the browser's own locale rules, so they read as they do everywhere else in the system
- The shop's language is resolved in one place instead of three copies that had already begun to disagree
- A token row lost a column nothing read: a one-time link is spent by deleting it, which is what made it one-time all along. Existing databases keep the column and are unaffected

1.15.1 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The feed gives the category tree as a tree: an element per segment tied by parentId, instead of one flat element named after the whole path. Yandex was reading a shop one level deep, and a catalogue copied between two instances of the shop landed beside the tree rather than inside it, as five new sections with the separator rewritten as a dash
- The catalogue fits a photo into its tile instead of cropping it. A marketplace card is a finished layout — the volume, the piece count and "gift wrapped" are printed on the image — and a square crop cut off exactly those lines
- A product description now runs the full width under both columns. Held inside the buy-box column it stretched that column alone, so a product with one photo showed half a page of nothing where the gallery ended
- The scrollbar under the main photo is gone; the gallery is paged by its thumbnails and by a finger

1.15.0 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop's own feed now states how many of each product are left, and import reads it. Copying a catalogue from one instance of the shop into another used to spread a single made-up number over everything, because the feed standard carries availability as a flag and no quantity at all: a product with one left and a product with twenty arrived identical, which is an oversell waiting for a buyer. A feed from anywhere else states no quantity and behaves exactly as before
- An import no longer keeps its coefficient as the shop's own. A shop that takes catalogues from several suppliers converts one of them out of another currency, and that number, remembered, repriced every other supplier's goods on their next import — by a factor of twenty-eight in the case that found it

1.14.2 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Wildberries sandbox no longer waits out a DNS timeout on every check: there is no common-api-sandbox host at all, so the test contour skips the seller-info call instead of falling back to production with a token that does not belong there. Found the first time a real test token was plugged in

1.14.1 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The admin showed roubles and the browser's language until the page was reloaded: the shop's own currency and the owner's language were fetched on a session that already existed, and reaching the admin through the login form skipped that. A Belarusian shop priced its goods in the wrong money for the whole first visit

1.14.0 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Import reads a supplier's own Excel price list, not only our CSV template. Columns are found by their headers and the header row by its content, so the logo, the phone number and three empty rows a price list opens with are no obstacle. Checked on a live 143 MB file: 23 699 products in five seconds
- Pictures pasted into the cells come out with their rows and are written straight to the shop's uploads. A photo inside a spreadsheet has no link to fall back on, so it is saved now or lost, and asking an owner to extract twenty thousand of them by hand is the same as saying no
- One file field, two formats: a spreadsheet is recognised by its own bytes, so there is no second radio button to think about
- The Wildberries tab, a channel of its own next to Ozon: connect the account, link products to cards by the seller's article, publish and unpublish by selection, set a platform price flat or by the markup ladder, sync stock and prices, and read the log of sales the platform made. Nothing is shared with Ozon except the ladder arithmetic — a channel is a vertical slice, and the differences below are why
- Linking reads both platform keys off the card: stock is set per barcode, price per nmID. Our own catalogue never carries a barcode, and it never has to — a price list from Excel or a YML feed links by article exactly as it does on Ozon
- A card with several sizes has several barcodes and one article, so which size a product means is not knowable. Those are reported as unlinked instead of guessed at, and only a catalogue imported from Wildberries itself (whose articles carry the size) links straight through
- Prices on Wildberries are accepted as an upload task and confirmed later, so "sent" and "accepted" are two different facts: the tab shows how many prices are in flight, a confirmed task becomes the new baseline, and a task the platform never answers releases its products after an hour instead of pinning them forever
- Sizes of one card that want different prices push nothing at all and say why. Wildberries takes one price per card, and picking one size's number would quietly reprice the others
- A sandbox switch in the settings: the tab talks to the Wildberries test hosts with a test token, so the connection can be proven without touching real goods, orders or balance
- The platform's own message is truncated and the token is stripped out of it before it reaches a log or the database: an API that echoes a request back would otherwise carry the key into our files
- Consecutive calls are spaced out. Wildberries meters per method, and a burst costs a 429 that lasts far longer than the pause
- Errors now carry the time of the next attempt. A failure with no "and then what" reads as if the sync had given up

1.13.2 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A photo still hosted on the supplier's server is marked with a warning badge, in the product list and in the product dialog alike. Imported photos are links, and a link dies with whoever holds it; until now nothing on the screen told the owner which pictures they do not actually own a copy of
- A product with no photo shows the storefront's own "no photo" stub in the admin list instead of a blank gap, so the column stays a column and the owner sees what a shopper would see
- The badge explains itself: as soon as one is on screen, a line above the table says what it means and that "Fill in" is what brings the photos in. A mark nobody can decode is a decoration

1.13.1 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The import screen says out loud whose photos and descriptions a feed carries, and asks the owner to confirm they may use them before the button works. It arrives with an Excel price list and a marketplace account just as it does with a feed, and it is also what makes the import an instruction from the owner rather than something we did to their shop

1.13.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Categories tab: the storefront's sections are now yours to keep. Create a section (including an empty one, before anything is in it), rename it, move it under another parent, set the order of siblings, hide a branch from the storefront, delete it. The tree is on the left, the form on the right — the layout every catalogue admin uses
- Renaming or moving a section carries its products and its whole branch with it, in one transaction, and the old address answers 301 instead of 404: tidying the tree must not throw away what a page earned in search
- Deleting a section is not deleting what stands on it: the products and the subcategories move up to the parent, and a root section's goods simply lose their category
- A hidden section and everything below it disappears from the catalogue, the category index and the sitemap
- Two names that would produce one storefront address are refused with a message instead of one page quietly replacing the other
- A buyer leaves a phone or an email, whichever they prefer: the checkout asks for both and refuses only when neither is filled — an order nobody can be reached about is a lost sale that looks like a sale. An email also gets the buyer a confirmation letter, so a shop that took an order no longer answers with silence
- The cart row got the controls every shop has: minus, plus and a cross. Until now it had an "Update" button and deleting a line meant typing a zero into the quantity — a rule nobody guesses, in the one place where a shop loses a sale it already had
- Orders can be deleted, by an explicit list of rows and with a confirmation that says what disappears. Stock is not returned by a delete — an order that still holds goods should be cancelled first. The email is in the admin list and in the CSV journal
- A text of your own on a category page: it shows above the goods and becomes the page description for search engines, instead of the same generated sentence on every page
- The category field in the product dialog took a quarter of a line and cut the path off; it now has a row of its own

1.12.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A page for every category: /c/tekstil/spalnya/kpb-evro with its own title, description, breadcrumbs and listing. A search for "купить КПБ евро оптом" lands on a page about that, not on the catalogue with a query parameter; the old ?category= address redirects to the new one permanently
- Categories now arrive with the catalogue. Every source fills them: the tree of a YML feed, the Ozon taxonomy (one request per import, not one per card), the WB subject directory, and a price-list cell written as "Текстиль > Спальня". Until now the column existed and no importer ever wrote to it — a shop of 20,000 products had one category page, empty
- A category is a path, so a parent shows everything below it: "Текстиль" lists the goods of "Текстиль/Спальня/КПБ", which gives the shop landing pages for broad searches as well as narrow ones
- Category pages and the category index are in the sitemap, each with its own lastmod
- Filters for the buyer, still without a line of JavaScript: sort by price or name, "in stock only". The state lives in the address, so a filtered list can be sent to someone; every variant points a canonical at the plain page, so search engines see one page and not five
- HEAD returned 405 for every storefront page: a GET-only route on the outer router answered before the storefront could. Monitoring, link checkers and some crawlers saw a shop that looked broken

1.11.0 / August 14, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Legal details for the shop: a free-form block in the Profile that goes into the footer of every storefront page and into schema.org/Organization markup, so search engines and buyers can see whom they are dealing with
- A "Delivery and payment" page: delivery, payment and returns written as plain text in the Profile, published at /info, linked from the footer and listed in the sitemap. Neither Yandex nor Google accepts a shop into shopping results without published terms; an empty field means no page and no link
- Product feeds for Yandex (/yml.xml) and Google Merchant Center (/gmc.xml)
- Search engines and analytics: Google Analytics and Yandex Metrika ids in the Profile, and the storefront still carries no script at all while both are empty
- Order emails come from the shop, not from a mailbox: the sender name is the shop name, and the address can differ from the SMTP login for owners whose provider forbids sending as another user
- A product with no photos shows a placeholder instead of an empty column half a page tall
- Instance stats in the admin: CPU, memory, disks, network, uptime, the size of the database and of the uploads directory
- New shops are handed a one-time invite link instead of a printed password
- An uploaded logo larger than needed is shrunk on upload
- The storefront's html lang follows the shop language
- Search-engine verification fields are gone from the Profile: proving domain ownership is the owner's business, and every registrar and search engine offers a way that needs no field in the shop
- The admin keeps one page width across tabs, and a product dialog scrolls itself instead of the page behind it

1.10.0 / August 10, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The catalogue grid shows small copies of the photos instead of the supplier's originals: a page of sixty products used to pull megabytes to draw thumbnails. Measured on a real catalogue, a 197 KB photo becomes 16 KB. The product page still shows the original
- Photos already in a shop get their small copies from the second stage of "Fill in", with the same progress and the same stop button
- Uploaded files are served with a long, immutable cache: a repeat visitor downloads no photos at all
- The admin panel had the storefront's icon in its browser tab — the seller's initial, generated for their shop — because it asked for /favicon.svg by absolute path

